  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .approach-table-scroll { display: none; }
    .approach-cards { display: block; }
    .approach-card {
      border: 1px solid #1a1a1a;
      border-top: 2px solid #e00;
      background: #050505;
      padding: 1.25rem;
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }
    .approach-card-name { color: #fff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 1rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
    .approach-card-row { color: #ccc; font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
    .approach-card-row span {
      color: #e00; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;
      display: block; margin-bottom: 0.15rem;
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 761px) and (max-width: 950px) {
    .approach-table { font-size: 0.7rem; }
    .approach-table th { font-size: 0.62rem; padding: 0.45rem 0.35rem; }
    .approach-table td { padding: 0.45rem 0.35rem; }
  }
</style>

<section style="padding: 3rem 0 5rem; background:#000; border-top:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.5rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size:1.6rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Which One Do You Actually Need?</h2>
    <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
      <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:0.85rem;color:#ddd;margin-bottom:1.5rem;min-width:480px;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
            <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.6rem;color:#fff;">If your situation is...</th>
            <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.6rem;color:#fff;">You need</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {scenarioRows.map(row => (
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
              <td style="padding:0.6rem;">{row.situation}</td>
              <td style="padding:0.6rem;">{row.need}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div style="border:1px solid #1a1a1a;border-left:2px solid #e00;background:#050505;padding:1.5rem 1.75rem;margin-bottom:2rem;">
      <p style="color:#888;font-size:0.7rem;letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:1rem;">A quick way to decide</p>
      <p style="color:#ddd;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin:0;">If you're still comparing design directions or colorways, and the fabric is a fairly standard weight and stretch — start digital. If you already know the design and the fabric has any unusual drape, stretch, or structure, or you're approving for bulk — go straight to physical. When in doubt, do both: digital first to catch the obvious problems cheaply, physical to confirm the parts digital can't.</p>
    </div>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">Why Drape and Hand-Feel Are the Hard Part</h3>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:2rem;">Fabric behavior is genuinely difficult to simulate accurately. A digital tool can assign approximate physical properties to a virtual fabric, but real fabric has inconsistencies, weight distribution, and stretch-recovery behavior that shift depending on cut, seams, and how it's actually sewn — details a simulation approximates rather than reproduces exactly. That gap matters most for fabrics with unusual characteristics: heavy structured outerwear, high-stretch technical fabrics, and anything where drape is central to how the garment is supposed to look and move. For a basic cotton t-shirt, the gap matters less. For a tailored jacket or a flowy dress, it matters a lot.</p>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">What a Simulation Actually Measures — and Why It Still Falls Short</h3>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:2rem;">Digital fabric simulation isn't guesswork — tools like CLO3D take real, measurable inputs: tensile strength (how the fabric stretches under tension), shear stiffness (how it distorts diagonally, which drives drape and pliability), bending stiffness (how easily it folds under its own weight), and basic weight and thickness. These are the same categories of properties textile labs have measured for decades using standardized fabric-testing equipment. The gap isn't that the software ignores physics — it's that a handful of averaged numbers can't fully capture how a specific bolt of fabric behaves once it's actually cut on the bias, sewn under real machine tension, and pressed. Small inconsistencies in the physical fabric, seam puckering from the sewing process, and fabric-specific creasing behavior are exactly the details a simulation approximates rather than reproduces.</p>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">A True 3D Sample Isn't the Same as an AI-Generated Image</h3>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:2rem;">Worth being precise about, since these get conflated constantly in 2026: an AI-generated product image can show a plausible-looking garment on a model, but it isn't built from real pattern pieces and doesn't simulate fabric physics at all — it's useful for a quick visual preview, not for validating fit or construction. A genuine 3D sample built in CLO3D or Browzwear starts from your actual pattern pieces, digitally stitched together, with fabric weight and stretch properties assigned, then simulated on an avatar. One is a picture. The other is an engineering step. Don't let a convincing AI-generated image substitute for either a real 3D simulation or a physical sample when you're actually validating a design.</p>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">Color on Screen Isn't a Guaranteed Match Either</h3>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:2rem;">This one catches buyers off guard as often as drape does. Modern 3D tools do integrate real Pantone color libraries, which helps — but the 3D viewport itself still displays in RGB, meaning a Pantone spec (built on physical, spectral color standards) gets converted for on-screen display. Add in that every monitor renders color slightly differently depending on its own calibration, and what looks like an exact match on a designer's screen can look noticeably different on a buyer's laptop, let alone against the actual dyed fabric. For any order where exact color matching matters — and for branded or repeat-order products, it usually does — confirm color against a physical fabric swatch or lab dip, not a screen render, digital or otherwise.</p>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">The Sustainability Angle Is Real, Not Just Marketing</h3>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;">Every physical sample round that gets rejected still consumed fabric, thread, labor, and international courier shipping — none of which is recoverable once the sample's been made and discarded. Catching an obvious fit or construction problem digitally, before cutting fabric, avoids that waste entirely for that round. It's not a reason to skip physical sampling altogether — the final approval sample still has to be made — but it's a genuine, non-marketing reason digital pre-sampling has gained real traction industry-wide, not just a talking point.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding: 3rem 0 5rem; background:#000; border-top:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.5rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size:1.6rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Common Mistakes — and the Myths Behind Them</h2>
    <div style="border:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
      <div style="padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
        <p style="color:#fff;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;"><span style="color:#e00;">Myth:</span> 3D sampling means you can skip physical samples entirely</p>
        <p style="color:#bbb;font-size:0.85rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0;"><span style="color:#888;">Fact:</span> It reduces how many physical rounds you need — reported at roughly one fewer round per style across the industry — but final approval before bulk production still needs a real physical sample. Fabric drape and hand-feel aren't reliably simulated yet.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
        <p style="color:#fff;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;"><span style="color:#e00;">Myth:</span> An AI-generated garment photo is basically the same as a 3D CLO sample</p>
        <p style="color:#bbb;font-size:0.85rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0;"><span style="color:#888;">Fact:</span> An AI image shows a plausible look; it doesn't simulate pattern or fabric behavior at all. A real 3D sample is built from your actual pattern pieces with fabric properties assigned — a validation step, not a preview picture.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;">
        <p style="color:#fff;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;"><span style="color:#e00;">Myth:</span> Every factory offering "digital sampling" means the same thing</p>
        <p style="color:#bbb;font-size:0.85rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0;"><span style="color:#888;">Fact:</span> Capability varies widely — some factories run true 3D pattern simulation, others offer basic renders or nothing at all. Ask specifically what tool and process they use rather than assuming.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-top:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
        <p style="color:#fff;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;"><span style="color:#e00;">Mistake:</span> Approving a design based only on a rendered image, digital or AI-generated</p>
        <p style="color:#bbb;font-size:0.85rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0;"><span style="color:#888;">Reality:</span> A rendered image, however polished, hasn't been tested against real fabric behavior. Treat any digital render — 3D-simulated or AI-generated — as a step toward a physical sample, not a substitute for approving one.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-top:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
        <p style="color:#fff;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;"><span style="color:#e00;">Mistake:</span> Not checking how the digital sample was graded across sizes</p>
        <p style="color:#bbb;font-size:0.85rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0;"><span style="color:#888;">Reality:</span> A design that looks right on one digital avatar size doesn't guarantee the grading rules translate cleanly across your full size range — confirm size-set fit physically, not just the sample size digitally.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div style="border:1px solid #1a1a1a;border-left:2px solid #e00;background:#050505;padding:1.5rem 1.75rem;margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <p style="color:#888;font-size:0.7rem;letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:1rem;">A pattern worth knowing about</p>
      <p style="color:#ddd;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin:0;">A pattern that comes up more than buyers expect: a design gets approved on screen looking exactly right, and then the physical sample arrives with a detail that never showed up in the simulation — a pocket that sags under the actual fabric's weight, a seam that puckers where the digital render assumed a perfectly even stitch. None of this means the digital step was wasted; it caught the silhouette and proportions early. It just means the physical sample was still doing a job the digital one couldn't.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding: 3rem 0 5rem; background:#000; border-top:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.5rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size:1.6rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Where SDF Stands — An Honest Self-Assessment</h2>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>What we can back up:</strong> our real strength is physical sample development — full detail on our <a href="/sample-service" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">sample service</a> and <a href="/sampling-service" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">sampling process</a> pages, including realistic costs ($30-200+ depending on garment type, often refundable against a bulk order) and turnaround (7-10 days plus shipping per round). Whichever sampling route you take, it starts with a solid <a href="/tech-pack-guide" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">tech pack</a> — see our guide if you're building one from scratch.</p>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;">
